## 4. The leaf pair-weight floor is normalized correctly

For retained terminal weights `a_tau,b_tau in[0,1]`,

\[
a_\tau+b_\tau\le1+\min(a_\tau,b_\tau).
\]

Summing inside one exact leaf phase gives

\[
z_i\le M_i+P_i+o(1).
\]

The independent near-frontier theorem gives `U,V<0.513-o(1)`.  Combining with the new
phasewise occurrence floor gives

\[
\boxed{P_U,P_V>0.2049-o(1).}
\]

No conversion from weighted mass to an unweighted train count occurs here.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 5. The common-center bad-weight bound is safe

Let

\[
x=CU-e(C,U),
\qquad y=CV-e(C,V),
\]

be the full missing center/leaf crossing masses.  The hostile-audited dominant-orbit
correlation gives

\[
\left(1-{V\over C}\right)x+
\left(1-{U\over C}\right)y
\le C(W-C)-E_{dom}.
\tag{HA.5}
\]

A retained O--O train with center degree at most half the finite frozen center family
misses at least `CD/2-o(D)` center trains.  Multiplying by its retained pair weight and
summing shows that the total bad retained weight in the two leaf families is at most

\[
{2(x+y)\over C}+o(1).
\]

Since `U>=V`, (HA.5) gives

\[
{2(x+y)\over C}
\le
{2[C(W-C)-E_{dom}]\over C-U}+o(1).
\]

Substitution of the safe bounds

\[
W<1.912456,
\qquad E_{dom}>0.90689967,
\qquad U<0.513
\]

is in the adverse direction.  The companion certificate reduces the remaining
one-variable inequality to

\[
q(C)=0.03405(C-0.513)
-2[C(1.912456-C)-0.90689967]>0.
\]

Its leading coefficient is positive and its exact discriminant is negative.  Hence

\[
\boxed{B_{bad}<0.03405-o(1).}
\]

Even if every bad unit of weight is charged to one leaf, both leaves retain good
weight

\[
\boxed{G_U,G_V>0.17085-o(1).}
\]

Every good train has degree strictly greater than half the same finite center set, so
every good U/V pair shares a center neighbor.

**Verdict:** PASS.

