## 6. Triangle-freeness forces the full good cross-product to be noncrossing

Take a good U-leaf O--O train and a good V-leaf O--O train.  Their center neighborhoods
both contain more than half of the center family, so they intersect.  A common center
train crosses both leaf trains in strict rhombi.

If the two leaf trains also crossed, these three trains would form a triangle in the
strict-rhombus train-crossing graph, forbidden throughout the triangle-free C-II
branch.  Thus every pair in the entire good U x V product is noncrossing.

The two leaf exact atoms have mutual projective phase separation `pi/3`: although their
oriented representatives can differ by `2pi/3`, projectively this is the same
`pi/3` separation.  Therefore (HA.4) applies uniformly to every pair in this product.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 7. Weighted product averaging has no hidden count/weight conversion

For every good pair,

\[
e_\tau+e_\sigma\ge\Lambda-o(1),
\qquad \Lambda=2-\sqrt3.
\]

Multiplication by `w_tau w_sigma` and summation gives

\[
G_VE_U^g+G_UE_V^g
\ge\Lambda G_UG_V-o(1).
\]

If `G_U<=G_V`, then nonnegativity gives

\[
G_VE_U^g+G_UE_V^g
\le G_V(E_U^g+E_V^g),
\]

so

\[
E_U^g+E_V^g\ge\Lambda G_U-o(1).
\]

The symmetric case is identical.  Thus

\[
E_U+E_V
\ge\Lambda\min(G_U,G_V)-o(1).
\]

The phase-window and Type-II geometric errors in the cross-phase endpoint theorem are
uniform after each window is frozen, so summing over `O(D^2)` weighted pairs and then
normalizing by `D^2` preserves an `o(1)` error.  No unweighted cardinality lower bound
is required.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 8. The final energy ledger is genuinely disjoint

For a leaf O--O train, the pair energy is weighted by

\[
w_\tau=\min(a_\tau,b_\tau)\le a_\tau,b_\tau,
\]

so `E_U+E_V` is a sub-sum of actual selected outer-edge directional energy in the two
leaf phase windows.  The center one-well energy `f(z_C)` uses only selected outer-edge
occurrences in the disjoint center phase window.  Therefore

\[
\delta\ge f(z_C)+E_U+E_V-o(1)
\]

adds physically disjoint energy contributions; it does not spend the detour budget
three times.

Using the safe rational bound `sqrt(3)<1.7321`, the certificate gives

\[
E_U+E_V>0.045770715-o(1),
\]

while

\[
f(0.7179)>0.0153173048.
\]

Thus

\[
\delta>0.0610880198-o(1).
\]

The envelope gives

\[
\delta<0.05034454+o(1).
\]

The contradiction margin exceeds `0.01074`, vastly larger than the earlier
phase-imbalance certification margin.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 9. Certificate and final disposition

The exact certificate is internally consistent with the analytic proof:

- Machin rational bounds enclose `pi`;
- integer-square-root bounds enclose `sqrt(3)` and `sqrt(Delta_2)`;
- alternating Taylor bounds are used in the correct outward direction for both `F`
  and the one-well phase energy;
- the `z=2.2196` retention boundary is certified inside the `h<0.00013` box;
- the phase-imbalance sign is positive;
- the common-center quadratic has negative discriminant;
- the final energy margin is certified above `0.01`.

The theorem therefore validly excludes the stated proof-only full-core Type-II branch
through

\[
\boxed{t\le1.8436.}
\]

This is not a closure of C-II or Level I.  It is a new lower-frontier exclusion at the
current full-core scope.

**Final verdict:** **PASS. C-II and Level I remain open.**
